# Programs whose card used to be built by a tools/playtests/*.keys script and
# a caption in gen_screens.py, now ordinary stanzas -- each with its own try
# line and a caption written for a stranger.  Screen toys and full-screen
# programs; every one ends with Ctrl-E from the harness whatever it is doing.
# backgammon and teachgammon draw their boards in one burst and are captured
# through an unthrottled path elsewhere; they are not here.

shot    worms
cap     Three worms, drawn in O, * and #, crawling about the screen at random.
cap     Control-C ends it.
try     worms
run     worms
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    rain
cap     Drops at several stages at once: a fresh one a dot, others opening out
cap     into rings of dashes and slashes.  Control-C ends it.
try     rain
run     rain
wait    6
snap
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
wait    4
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    sonnet
cap     Fourteen numbered lines of iambic pentameter, the words drawn at
cap     random from a vocabulary compiled into the program, and the prompt: m
cap     and u mark and unmark a line by number, r recomposes the unmarked
cap     lines around the ones you kept, w appends the poem to a file, q quits.
try     sonnet
run     sonnet
wait    4
snap
wait    3
snap
send    \e
wait    2

shot    sc
cap     A spreadsheet. Cells are named by column letter and row number; a
cap     leading `=' enters a number or formula in the current cell, the
cap     arrow keys or hjkl move, and `?' lists the commands. Here 42 has
cap     been entered in cell A0.
try     sc
run     export TERM=vt100
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     sc
wait    4
keys    =42
send    \r
wait    2
snap
send    q
wait    1
send    y
wait    2

shot    banner
cap     Peace, in seven-row letters.
try     banner Peace
run     banner Peace
wait    5

shot    banner1
cap     With `-s' each letter is built out of itself: Groovy in letters made of
cap     its own letters. -d doubles the size, -i slants it and -c= picks the
cap     character.
try     banner1 -s Groovy
run     banner1 -s Groovy
wait    5


shot    beav
cap     A binary editor and viewer, here on a file of the disk's own
cap     documentation: the offset, sixteen bytes in hexadecimal, and the
cap     same sixteen as characters. To edit, type hexadecimal digits: they
cap     replace the byte under the cursor, and the character column
cap     changes with them. Ctl-X Ctl-A switches between replacing and
cap     inserting. Ctl-X Ctl-E saves and leaves. Its own manual is
cap     DOC/beav.
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
try     beav README-CIO
run     beav README-CIO
wait    5
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    card
size    24 80
cap     A Towers of Hanoi solving itself, whose twelve disks are the lines
cap     of a season's greeting; the message assembles as the disks arrive on
cap     the far peg.
try     card
run     card
wait    16
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    nobs
cap     Cribbage against the computer.  It deals six cards, asks which two
cap     to lay away to the crib, then plays the hand and pegs the score on
cap     the board above.  Control-C leaves it.
try     nobs
run     nobs
wait    5
snap
wait    4
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    bite
cap     A skull draws itself, then bites; a screen toy.  `q' quits.
try     bite
run     bite
wait    4
snap
wait    3
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    life
cap     Conway's Game of Life on `glider' from GAMES/LIFE, which is a glider
cap     gun, with a glider already loose below it, a few generations in.
cap     The board is 79 by 23; any window at least 80 by 24 shows it whole.
cap     Control-C ends it.
try     life glider
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES/LIFE
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     life glider
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    tess
size    30 80
cap     The researcher's office where it starts, the stack you carry, and
cap     north into a green house.  Commands are two words, `look at' and a
cap     thing's name among them; `-f' skips the title and scenario.
try     tess -f
run     tess -f
wait    5
send    inventory\r
wait    3
send    n\r
wait    3
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    world
size    30 80
pages
cap     The opening, on a trail near the top of a pass, then `inventory' and
cap     `n', which climbs almost to the top.  The usual verbs move you and
cap     act; `quit' leaves.  It reads its text from GAMES/world.
try     world
run     clear
wait    1
run     world
wait    6
snap
send    inventory\r
wait    3
send    n\r
wait    4
snap
send    quit\r
wait    2
send    y\r
wait    2

shot    wish
size    24 80
cap     Started in GAMES: the directory in a window, its files listed, the one
cap     under the cursor described along the foot.  Type a command on the top
cap     line and Enter runs it.  The labels along the bottom are function keys
cap     a vt100 does not send, so use the control keys: Ctrl-P/N/B/F move the
cap     cursor, Ctrl-A marks a file and Ctrl-W puts its name on the command
cap     line, Ctrl-D leaves.
try     wish
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     wish
wait    8
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    cam
size    30 80
cap     Camshaft design: the dialogue for an intake lobe -- the rocker
cap     ratio, the valve lift at every twenty degrees of crank from -180 to
cap     180, and the base circle.  RETURN then plots the lift curve in
cap     Tektronix vectors, for a Tektronix graphics terminal.
try     cam
run     clear
wait    1
run     cam
wait    3
send    1.5\r
wait    1
send    0\r
wait    1
send    0\r
wait    1
send    0\r
wait    1
send    0\r
wait    1
send    .02\r
wait    1
send    .08\r
wait    1
send    .18\r
wait    1
send    .30\r
wait    1
send    .40\r
wait    1
send    .45\r
wait    1
send    .40\r
wait    1
send    .30\r
wait    1
send    .18\r
wait    1
send    .08\r
wait    1
send    .02\r
wait    1
send    0\r
wait    1
send    0\r
wait    1
send    0\r
wait    1
send    0\r
wait    1
send    1.2\r
wait    3
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    lissaj
cap     Draws Lissajous figures -- the curve two oscillators trace against
cap     each other -- from the frequencies, duration and phase you give it.
cap     The curve is drawn in Tektronix vectors; on a character terminal the
cap     dialogue is the readable part.
try     lissaj
run     lissaj
wait    4
send    3\r
wait    2
send    2\r
wait    2
send    0\r
wait    2
snap
send    1\r
wait    1
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    backgammon
size    30 80
burst
cap     Backgammon against the computer.  It asks whether you want the rules
cap     and the instructions, then which colour to take, and draws the board
cap     -- the points numbered 1 to 24, the bar down the middle, your men as
cap     `w' and the computer's as `r' -- then prompts for each move.  Here
cap     White's first roll and the Move: prompt; `q' and `y' leave the game.
try     backgammon
run     export TERM=vt100
run     clear
run     backgammon
send    n\r
send    n\r
send    w\r
send    q\r
send    y\r

shot    teachgammon
size    40 80
burst
cap     A backgammon tutor: it sets out the rules a screen at a time, and `t'
cap     goes straight to the tutorial, a practice game against the computer
cap     explained a move at a time.  Here its opening roll and first move, and
cap     the prompt for yours.  The `Error: 000:000 - readc' beside it is this
cap     card's typed input running out; at a keyboard it waits for your move.
try     teachgammon
run     export TERM=vt100
run     clear
run     teachgammon
send    \s\r
send    t\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
send    \s\r
